            <div class="col s12 m2"></div>
            <div class="col s12 m8 paragraphStyleNews">
                <p class="bolderFont">
                    He Never Missed a Shot, by Gleb Kuchuk and Erik Alens, takes the top prize as Vilpa Films prepares
                    to support the project through its next stage of production.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <span class="bolderFont">Vilpa Films has announced the winner and finalists of its 2026 Script to
                        Screen competition,</span> an international initiative hosted through FilmFreeway and created to
                    discover emerging filmmaking voices and help promising projects move beyond the page.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Taking this year’s top honor is <span class="bolderFont">He Never Missed a Shot</span>, by <span
                        class="bolderFont">Gleb Kuchuk and Erik Alens</span>. The project
                    was selected following a competitive evaluation process that considered not only the strength of the
                    screenplay, but the wider filmmaking potential behind the project.
                </p>
                <p>As the winner of Script to Screen 2026, He Never Missed a Shot will now receive support from Vilpa
                    Films as the team works toward bringing the project into production.</p>
                <p>
                    For Vilpa Films, that next step is at the heart of what the competition was designed to accomplish.
                    Script to Screen was conceived not simply as another screenwriting award, but as a way of
                    identifying filmmakers at an important stage in their careers and creating tangible opportunities
                    for their work to progress.
                </p>
                <p>
                    “Great scripts deserve more than recognition — they deserve a pathway forward,” said <span
                        class="bolderFont">Alejandro
                        Vilpa, Founder & CEO of Vilpa Films</span>. “What interested us about this competition was the
                    possibility
                    of finding filmmakers we genuinely wanted to continue working with. With He Never Missed a Shot, we
                    found a project and a filmmaking team that we are very excited to support as they move into the next
                    chapter.”
                </p>
                <h4 class="bolderFont">
                    An Exceptional Group of Finalists
                </h4>
                <p>While He Never Missed a Shot ultimately received the top prize, Vilpa Films noted that the strength
                    of this year’s finalist selection made the final decision particularly difficult.</p>
                <p>The <span class="bolderFont">Script to Screen 2026 finalists</span> were:</p>
                <p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Infant Esau — Matison Leblanc</li>
                    <li>A Good Man in Hell — Sador Zerie</li>
                    <li>Inherited — Sabrina Revelle</li>
                    <li>What Are You Doing Sunday? — William Travers</li>
                    <li>Algorithm — Sheree M. Lewis</li>
                    <li>The Echoe of Love — Qibing Zhang</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p>Across genres, styles and perspectives, the shortlisted projects demonstrated an unusually high level
                    of ambition and individuality. The finalists stood out not simply for polished screenwriting, but
                    for the presence of distinct creative voices behind their work.</p>
                <p>Vilpa Films described the group as one of the most exciting outcomes of the competition itself.</p>
                <p>
                    “The finalists genuinely exceeded our expectations,” Vilpa added. “There were scripts with very
                    different tones, worlds and creative ambitions, but what they shared was a sense that there was a
                    filmmaker behind the writing — someone with something specific to say. Several of these projects
                    stayed with us long after we finished reading them.”
                </p>
                <p>Rather than treating the finalist announcement as the end of the relationship, Vilpa Films is also
                    beginning conversations with members of the group about their current and upcoming projects, with
                    the intention of identifying potential opportunities for future collaboration.</p>
                <h4 class="bolderFont">
                    From Script to Production
                </h4>
                <p>
                    With the competition concluded, Vilpa Films will begin working with Gleb Kuchuk and Erik Alens as
                    the project advances toward production.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The selection was influenced by more than the screenplay alone. The company considered the story,
                    execution, filmmaking vision and the broader potential of the creative team behind it — a
                    combination that ultimately distinguished the project within an exceptionally competitive group.
                </p>
                <p>The result is precisely what Script to Screen was created to achieve: <span
                        class="bolderFont">discover a compelling emerging
                        voice, recognize the work, and then help create the conditions for that work to exist on
                        screen.</span></p>
                <p>For the finalists, meanwhile, the competition has opened the door to an ongoing dialogue with Vilpa
                    Films rather than simply providing a laurel or placement.</p>
                <p>And for Vilpa Films, the caliber of the inaugural group has reinforced the value of continuing to
                    search for filmmakers whose careers may only be beginning.</p>
                <p class="bolderFont">Script to Screen 2026 may have selected one winner, but it introduced Vilpa Films to a much larger
                    group of voices worth following.</p>

                <h4 class="bolderFont" style="color: black !important;">
